.TH HWDP 1 "2026" "hwdp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hwdp \- hardware display-profile detection, layout, and autoscale suite
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hwdp
recognises which monitors are present and lays out / autoscales the workspace
for them, keyed on the HARDWARE (an EDID-derived display-profile id), never on
a hostname. It installs ONE command,
.BR hwdp ,
whose subcommands are executables under libexec/cmd/, so adding one is
adding a file, plus the standalone clients
.B run-scaled
and
.B wallpaper-slicer
described below. It is environment-agnostic: it works under X11 or a wlroots
compositor, and hardcodes no downstream tool: everything session-specific
is a HOOK or a PROVIDER an integrator fills.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B hwdp id " [--panels]"
The display-profile id for the connected monitor set: a compound hash over the
connected connectors' EDIDs, so it names the SET regardless of order or which
connector each panel sits on. Empty with nothing connected. Resolves HEADLESS.
.IP
.B --panels
asks the same question at a different granularity, printing
.I "<connector> <panel-id>"
for each connected panel: the set id says which DESK you are at, a panel id
says which MONITOR this is. A consumer storing PER-PANEL state needs the
second, since a connector name is the least stable thing in a docking setup
(which port, which MST hub) while the panel it names is what the state is
actually about. A panel id is TRUNCATED, so no set id can be rebuilt from
panel ids and neither granularity can be derived from the other. Two
identical panels share an EDID and therefore a panel id, which is a property
of EDID rather than of this projection.
.TP
.B hwdp shape
The workspace SHAPE: what KIND of desk is attached. One token per display,
.B B
(built-in: an eDP, LVDS or DSI connector) or
.B E
(external), then
.B L
or
.B P
(landscape or portrait, after rotation), joined with
.B +
and sorted:
.BR BL ", " EL+EP ", " BL+EL+EP ", " EP+EP+EP .
Sorted rather than left to right, so rearranging panels does not change it.
A switched-off panel is not a token. The grid, workspace names, window rules
and wallpaper key on it. Resolves HEADLESS too, from the kernel's native
modes, which carry no rotation: there a portrait-mounted panel reads
.BR L .
(It answered
.BR single " or " triple ,
a panel count, until 2026-10-29.)
.TP
.B hwdp ui
Per-display UI sizing as shell-sourceable KEY=VALUE
.RB ( KITTY_FONT ", " TITLE_FONT ", " MAKO_FONT ", " LOCK_RADIUS ", "
.BR CURSOR_SIZE ),
for apps whose size is a single global config and so cannot self-scale per
output. An optional
.I <id>.ui
override wins per key; else a density default. Resolves HEADLESS, and REFUSES
(exit 3, no output) rather than guess when no display answers at all.
.TP
.B hwdp geometry
Print one line per enabled output:
.I NAME MODE_W MODE_H TRANSFORM SCALE POS_X POS_Y LOG_W LOG_H ORIENTATION
.I MM_W MM_H:
projected from the shared layout probe. Settles by default (samples until
the layout holds still);
.B \-\-now
prints one snapshot.
.IP
MM_W/MM_H are the panel's PHYSICAL size from the EDID, and are 0 when it does
not report one (some do not, and some lie), so treat 0 as unknown rather than
as a measurement. They swap with a 90/270 transform, since the EDID describes
the panel. They matter because logical pixels are not a physical measure: 1920
of them span 530mm on one panel and 3840 span 590mm on another, so an image
laid out across the LOGICAL union is continuous in pixels while jumping 1.8x in
physical size at the bezel. Anything spanning outputs as ONE image needs the
millimetres. Fields are APPENDED, never inserted, so a consumer's field numbers
survive.
.TP
.B hwdp panels
One line per CONNECTED panel, \fB<connector> <native_w> <native_h>\fR. The
counterpart to
.BR geometry ,
and the split is the one this package is built on: panels is what the KERNEL
sees attached (DRM sysfs, so it answers from a TTY, at boot, at a greeter, over
ssh), geometry is how a COMPOSITOR has arranged it and needs a session. Use it
to size anything drawn BEFORE a session exists (a boot menu, a greeter)
where the compositor's current mode does not yet exist and the panel's native
mode is the right question. The EDID hash the provider carries is deliberately
NOT emitted;
.B hwdp id
is the published way to ask for identity, and
.B hwdp id --panels
is that question per panel. Nothing connected is empty output at
status 0, an answer rather than an error.
.TP
.B hwdp layout
Emit the runtime kanshi config for the connected set and print its path: inject
each output's sticky scale into the profile named by the id, or synthesize a
side-by-side one when there is none. An authored profile records the space each
output occupied when it was captured; when a scale has changed since, the
outputs are RE-PLACED for the new sizes, keeping which sits beside which and
how each is aligned along the shared edge, and the change is reported on
stderr. A position moved by hand switches that off for the profile. It then
checks that every output shares an EDGE with the rest, with no gap and no
overlap, and WARNS on stderr if not; the config is still written. A
hand-written or hand-moved profile is where a gap opened by a scale change
shows up. Edge-flipping treats an edge as interior only when a neighbour's edge
sits at exactly the same coordinate, so even a one-pixel gap makes the pointer
flip the workspace instead of crossing.
.TP
.B hwdp capture
Snapshot the current arrangement (transforms, positions, modes: no scale)
into a profile named by the id, so a rig is onboarded by arranging it once.
A panel that is attached but switched off is written as
.BI "output " name " disable" ,
because kanshi applies a profile only when it names every connected output.
It REFUSES, writing nothing, when the outputs do not meet edge to edge, and
names the outputs and the distance.
.TP
.B hwdp apply " [" \-q ]
Fire the
.I changed
hooks now. Every other path into them is driven by a DISPLAY event, but the
answers this tool gives also depend on things no display event announces: a
per-rig
.IR <id>.ui " override, or the calibrated sizing itself."
Nothing watches those, so the hooks never run, and a consumer that CACHES its
answer keeps serving the stale one. Hooks are expected idempotent, so this is
safe to run at any time; a failing hook is reported and the rest still run, with
a non-zero overall status. It does NOT regenerate the config or signal
.BR kanshi
 (that is the supervisor's job) it re-runs the REACTIONS only.
.B \-q
says nothing on success, for a provisioning run.
.TP
.BR "hwdp arrange" " [" \-\-dry\-run "] " \fIPLACEMENT\fR...
Write this desk's layout from placements, then apply it. Each output is placed
against ONE already placed, with its own alignment along that edge, so a mixed
desk is a list:
.IP
.B "hwdp arrange 'DP-1 rotate 90' 'DP-2 right-of DP-1 align bottom'"
.B "  'eDP-1 below DP-2 align center'"
.IP
A placement is
.IR "out " [ "rotate 0|90|180|270" ]
then nothing (the first, the anchor),
.IR "right-of|left-of|above|below out " [ "align A" ],
.I "at X,Y"
(an exact position in logical pixels, for fine placement; it must still meet
its neighbours edge to edge), or
.BR off .
Beside a neighbour A is
.BR top ", " center " or " bottom
(default top); above or below one,
.BR left ", " center " or " right
(default left). Without
.B rotate
an output keeps its rotation. Every placement shares an edge by construction.
Every switched-on output must be placed or switched off (kanshi applies a
profile only when it names every connected output); one already off and not
mentioned stays off. An output is a connector, read once at that moment, or a
quoted EDID name. CONNECTORS ARE NOT STABLE (a dock or hub renumbers them), so
the profile written is keyed by EDID name, and a placement list kept to replay
later should use EDID names too. It writes the same profile
.B hwdp capture
does (the old one kept as .bak), regenerates the layout and nudges a running
supervisor; with none running it says so instead of starting one. Refusals
(an output left out, two in one space, an unknown name, a bad alignment) exit
2 and write nothing.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the placements and the profile, and writes nothing.
.TP
.BR "hwdp profile" " [" \-\-missing ]
The authored layout profile for the connected set: its path, exit 0, or exit
1 when there is none. A desk NEEDS one when
.B HWDP_MAP_MIN
(2) or more panels are attached and none names the set; one panel has nothing
to arrange.
.B \-\-missing
answers that as a reminder's recheck wants it: 0 still needed, 1 not, 2 when
it cannot tell (no displays). Headless.
.TP
.BR "hwdp surfaces" " [" \-\-check ]
This desk's named SURFACES, resolved against the outputs switched on now, one
line each:
.IP
.I "SURFACE CONNECTOR WS_X WS_Y X Y W H"
.IP
X Y W H is the surface's rect INSIDE that output, in its logical pixels.
A surface names a panel (by its EDID name, as a kanshi profile does), a part of
it
.RB ( top ", " bottom ", " left ", " right " or " full ,
halves) and a workspace cell, in
.IR ~/.config/hwdp/surfaces/<id>.conf :
.IP
.B surface comms """Dell Inc. U2720Q ABC123""" cell 0 0 bottom
.IP
hwdp owns which part of which panel a name means at a desk, since only it can
turn a fraction of a panel into pixels at the scale in force; what a name means
for WINDOWS is the consumer's. No file prints nothing and exits 0. A file that
does not parse, uses a name twice or lets two surfaces claim the same space is
refused whole (exit 2, nothing on stdout), since a half-right map seeds windows
into the wrong places. A surface on a switched-off panel is skipped and named
on stderr. Needs a session (exit 3 without one).
.B \-\-check
describes each surface in words and notes a panel name that matches nothing
attached, and a switched-on panel no surface uses.
.TP
.BR "hwdp map" " [" \-\-width
.IR N ]
Draw this desk in text: the switched-on outputs where the compositor has them
(logical size, after rotation and scale), each labelled with its connector,
shape token and size; this desk's surfaces drawn into their halves (split with
dots, so a region cannot be mistaken for a panel edge); then a legend, the
shape, and any gap or overlap as a PROBLEM line. A report, safe at any time.
A broken surfaces file is reported and not drawn. Needs a session.
.TP
.B hwdp watch
Supervise the layout for a session (supervise-or-nudge via
.BR flock ),
regenerate the config through
.BR "hwdp layout" ,
and re-apply on compositor-config changes. It fires display-change HOOKS from
.IR <root>/<edge>.d/* " (edges: " pre ", " changed ", " unmapped ", " mapped )
so an integrator wires the reactions; it knows nothing of any specific
compositor tool.
.PP
It reacts to the DISPLAY SET changing as well as to the compositor config: the
connected panels are polled every
.B HWDP_HOTPLUG_POLL
seconds (default 4, 0 disables), and on a change the kanshi config is
REGENERATED before the hooks run and kanshi reloads: its profiles describe
the outputs that existed when it was written, so a reload alone would re-read a
file that does not mention the new panel. The fingerprint is the same probe
.B hwdp id
hashes, so a monitor swapped on one connector counts even though that connector
never leaves `connected'. Hook roots:
.BR HWDP_HOOK_ROOT " (default ~/.config/hwdp/hooks), " HWDP_MACHINE_HOOKS
(default /etc/hwdp/hooks).
.PP
The same poll is the
.B kanshi
watchdog: a kanshi that dies is restarted within one interval, with or without
an event to wake the supervisor. And the supervisor touches only ITS OWN
session's kanshi, one whose environment carries the same
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR " and " WAYLAND_DISPLAY .
It adopts, reloads and stops no other, so a supervisor in a nested compositor,
a second seat or a test sandbox cannot reach the desktop's.
.PP
The supervisor DETACHES, so its own diagnostics go to
.BR HWDP_WATCH_LOG ,
default $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hwdp/watch.log, truncated per supervisor and gone on
reboot. Set it to /dev/null to opt out. It is where the hotplug announcements,
"kanshi gone, restarting" and any failing hook are recorded: a background
process that reports nothing cannot be debugged after the fact, and display
faults almost always are.
.SH MIXED DPI
Panels of different physical density need
.BR HWDP_TARGET_PPI .
.B hwdp ui
emits one value per key because each consumer has one global config (kitty a
single font_size, pixdecor a single compositor-wide title_font, mako a single
font) so there is nowhere to put a per-output value. The only fix is to
remove the difference: set
.B HWDP_TARGET_PPI=<n>
and
.B hwdp layout
scales each output, from its EDID millimetre size, so every one lands near that
LOGICAL density. One set of numbers is then correct everywhere, and
.B hwdp ui
sizes for the target rather than for any one panel.
.PP
The cost is logical desktop AREA, not sharpness: with
wp_fractional_scale_manager_v1 a 4K at 1.75 still draws text at 1.75x device
pixels; what shrinks is the logical desktop (3840x2160 to 2194x1234).
.PP
Left unset, every output stays at scale 1 and density is absorbed by the UI
numbers instead: exact for one panel or a wall of identical ones. Seeing
panels more than 25% apart,
.B hwdp ui
then names them with their ppi on stderr, sizes for the DENSEST (oversized on a
coarse panel is clumsy but readable; the reverse is microscopic), still emits a
full key set and still exits 0.
.PP
A scale below 1 is always clamped to 1.00 and reported: it cannot render, so
writing it would make the config claim a scale the session never has.
.SH CLIENTS
Two further commands ship alongside
.BR hwdp ,
deliberately NOT subcommands of it: every subcommand above reads or manages
display state and returns, where these CONSUME that state to do something else.
Both read
.B "hwdp geometry"
or
.B "hwdp ui"
as ordinary clients, exactly as an integrator's own tool would.
.TP
.B run-scaled
Takes someone else's command line and BECOMES that process, so its exit status
and stdout are the child's. It magnifies a single application via a nested
.B gamescope
window (renders at a size/scale and upscales), windowed or fullscreen, for
fixed-layout programs that cannot scale themselves. The default magnification
comes from
.BR "hwdp ui" 's
.B MAGNIFY
key, so the factor is a property of the DISPLAY rather than something each
caller hardcodes.
.TP
.B wallpaper-slicer
Cuts one image into per-output crops that TILE across a multi-output layout.
Wayland clients that draw a wallpaper (swaylock, swaybg, a greeter background)
paint the same image on every output and have no spanned mode, so the source is
pre-sliced: each crop is that output's sub-rectangle of the image after it is
cover-scaled (fill) or contained (fit) over the whole layout union. Every slice
comes from one global cover rect, so a feature crossing a bezel stays
continuous. Slices are cut in LOGICAL orientation and never pre-rotated:
the compositor applies the output transform. Prints
.IR NAME <TAB> PATH
per output; needs one of
.BR magick ", " convert " or " vips .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hwdp/hooks/<edge>.d/*
User display-change hooks run by `hwdp watch'.
.TP
.I ~/.config/kanshi/{profiles,auto-scale}
Layout templates and the sticky per-panel scale overrides.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hwdp/providers/{layout,panels}/*
User display probe providers, tried before the machine and shipped ones.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hwdp/surfaces/<id>.conf
This desk's named surfaces (see
.BR "hwdp surfaces" ),
one file per display profile id.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HWDP_HOOK_ROOT ", " HWDP_MACHINE_HOOKS
Roots for `hwdp watch's display-change hooks.
.TP
.B HWDP_PROVIDER_ROOT ", " HWDP_MACHINE_PROVIDERS
Roots for display probe providers. Each holds a
.I layout
and/or
.I panels
directory whose executables are tried in filename order; the first to exit 0
with output wins, and one that cannot answer in this environment exits
non-zero and is skipped.
.TP
.B HWDP_UNMAPPED_SETTLE
Seconds the connected set must hold still before
.B hwdp watch
fires
.B unmapped
(default 30). Each hotplug restarts it, so monitors plugged in one at a time
are announced once, for the desk that results.
.TP
.B HWDP_MAP_MIN
How many attached panels make a desk need a profile (default 2).
.TP
.B HWDP_SURFACES
The directory holding the surface files (default
.IR ~/.config/hwdp/surfaces ).
.TP
.B HWDP_GEOM_BACKEND
An executable tried as the first layout provider, ahead of every directory.
.TP
.B HWDP_DRM
The DRM sysfs root the shipped panels provider reads (default
.IR /sys/class/drm ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kanshi (1),
.BR wlr-randr (1),
.BR gamescope (1)
